reasoning:
I copied each solution to a fresh temp directory and ran its tests: Z passed 106/106, Y 83/83 and X 25/25. I then ran the same edge-case probe against each running server.mjs and tried each UI in a browser.

Y ranks first. It met the spec on every probe except one documented corner (a DELETE with a code that fails the pattern gets 404 before the token check). Its input hardening is the best of the three: control characters rejected, non-ASCII redirect targets encoded, a memory-bounded limiter, a paused body on 413, and PORT and start-error handling. Its tests cover those hardening cases, plus a frontend round trip against a real server.

Z ranks second. It has the cleanest design, the largest and best-layered test suite, and an excellent README. But it has a real bug in the core redirect path: a URL with non-ASCII characters (or an embedded control character) is accepted with 201, and redirecting to it returns 500, because the raw url is written into the Location header. Y and X both handle that case.

X ranks third. It meets the spec's main requirements and even handles non-ASCII redirects, but it is the least robust:
- the limiter does an O(n) sweep on every request and keeps unbounded per-IP arrays;
- an oversized chunked body is drained in full, and the 413 does not close the connection;
- PORT is not validated and listen errors are not handled;
- control-character URLs are stored as sent but redirected to a normalized URL.

Its UI is minimal, its tests are much thinner (no chunked 413, no limiter unit tests, and a CLI test on a random port that can collide), and its README is terse.
